<h2 id="slowest">Slowest tests</h2>

<p>
    Where the time actually goes. Screenshot comparison and axe both drive a real
    browser over a whole page, so they dominate — which is the argument for
    keeping the visual suite pointed at one styleguide page and a handful of
    sample routes instead of every route on the site.
</p>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th scope="col">Test</th>
            <th scope="col">Spec</th>
            <th scope="col">Project</th>
            <th scope="col">Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        {% for test in testRuns.latest.slowest %}
        <tr>
            <th scope="row">{{ test.title }}</th>
            <td><code>{{ test.file }}</code></td>
            <td>{{ test.project }}</td>
            <td class="numeric" data-volatile>{{ (test.ms / 1000) | round(1) }}s</td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="coverage">What the suite covers</h2>

<p>
    Routes are discovered from the build output rather than listed in a spec, so
    a page added next month is covered by every suite without anyone remembering
    to add it. These counts come from that discovery, not from a tally kept by
    hand.
</p>

<dl class="run-summary">
    <div class="run-summary__item">
        <dt>Pages built</dt>
        <dd class="run-summary__figure" data-volatile>{{ testRuns.latest.coverage.routes }}</dd>
    </div>
    <div class="run-summary__item">
        <dt>Content pages</dt>
        <dd class="run-summary__figure" data-volatile>{{ testRuns.latest.coverage.contentPages }}</dd>
    </div>
    <div class="run-summary__item">
        <dt>In the sitemap</dt>
        <dd class="run-summary__figure" data-volatile>{{ testRuns.latest.coverage.indexablePages }}</dd>
    </div>
    <div class="run-summary__item">
        <dt>axe scans</dt>
        <dd class="run-summary__figure" data-volatile>{{ testRuns.latest.coverage.axeRuns }}</dd>
    </div>
</dl>

<p>
    Pages built is larger than content pages because redirect stubs and the 404
    are held to their own rules and checked separately. The axe count is every
    content page in both themes at both viewports, which is where most of the run
    time in the table above comes from.
</p>

{% else %}

<h2 id="the-run">No run recorded yet</h2>

<p>
    This page renders a record written by the test suite on a CI runner. None has
    been accepted yet, so there is nothing to show — and showing a number anyway,
    from a run on a laptop, is the specific failure this page is built to avoid.
</p>

<p>
    A record produced locally cannot be honest here. The visual suite compares
    against baselines generated on Ubuntu, and Windows rasterises text
    differently enough that a local run reports failures which are a font
    difference rather than a defect. So the record has to come from the runner,
    and <code>tests/test-report.spec.js</code> refuses to let one that did not
    reach the site.
</p>

{% endif %}

<h2 id="gates">How this site is gated</h2>

<p>
    A quality-focused Lighthouse CI run holds every pull request to fixed
    budgets, not targets. Exceed one and the build fails. That is the same
    discipline described in the case studies, applied to this site's own
    delivery.
</p>

<ul>
    <li>{% metric "a11y_budgets" %}</li>
    <li>{% metric "perf_budgets" %}</li>
    <li>{% metric "weight_budgets" %}</li>
</ul>

<p>
    Per the Web Almanac's 2025 crawl, most of the web fails the contrast and
    focus-visibility checks this site's accessibility budget requires on every
    route, in both themes, at both a desktop and a mobile viewport.
</p>

<p>
    The budgets and the suite block at different points, on purpose. HTML
    validation and the Playwright suite run on both the pull request and the
    deploy, because two changes that each pass alone can produce a duplicate
    title or a dead link once merged, and nothing re-checks that unless the
    deploy does. Lighthouse blocks pull requests only. A performance regression
    is worth arguing about before it merges, but on the deploy path it would let
    a shared runner having a slow minute stop a one-line copy fix from reaching
    production.
</p>

<h2 id="not-here">What is not here</h2>

<p>
    <strong>A flake rate.</strong> <code>playwright.config.js</code> sets retries
    to zero, so a test either passes or fails and there is no retry data to
    derive a flake rate from. I could publish one by turning retries on and
    counting second attempts, and until I do, this page does not get to imply a
    number it cannot produce. Zero retries is also the honest setting for a suite
    this size: a retry that turns red green is a bug that ships.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>A coverage percentage.</strong> These are end-to-end assertions over
    built HTML, not unit tests over branches. A line-coverage figure for a static
    site would measure the wrong thing precisely.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Per-test history.</strong> The trend is run-level: totals, failures
    and duration. Tracking every test's result over time would need a store that
    outlives a commit, and the honest version of that is a database rather than a
    file in a repository. Until there is a reason to run one, the trend says what
    a file can say.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>A live result.</strong> The counts above are the previous run, not one
    that started when you opened this page. This site is static files on GitHub
    Pages: there is no server here to run a suite on request. The page could fetch
    the current status from an API instead, and that would cost this site the one
    claim it is most careful about — every request on a published page resolving
    to its own origin. A link to the run is worth more than a badge, and costs
    nothing.
</p>

<h2 id="pipeline">How the record gets here</h2>

<p>
    Playwright writes a JSON report,
    <code>scripts/test-report.mjs</code> reduces it to the few fields this page
    renders, prepends it to the run history, and the result is committed. The
    reducer refuses to write a report holding too few tests to be the whole
    suite, so running one spec file and publishing the outcome cannot happen
    quietly. It also refuses when a spec file has no description recorded for it,
    because a row a reader has to guess at is worse than no row, and when
    Playwright's own totals disagree with its per-test records, which means the
    shape this script reads has moved under it.
</p>

<p>
    The workflows upload that file as an artifact rather than committing it back.
    A workflow that pushes to <code>main</code> on every deploy triggers itself,
    needs write permission the job does not have, and turns a published figure
    into something nobody chose to publish. It is the same arrangement as the
    visual baselines: the machine produces it, a person accepts it.
</p>
